        en: "The related concept of embedding the rules of a game to improve its design is treated in an earlier paper [7]. While there is overlap between the two concepts, embedding the rules is actually a subset of the broader aim of making the design do the work, which can take other forms such as even increasing the complexity of rule sets in order to benefit the player. Embedding the rules aims to minimise the number of rules that players must learn, while making the design do the work aims to minimise the mental effort that players must expend in order to play the game. This is the difference between the clarity of a rule set (form) and the clarity of moves in action and their implications (function) [19].",
